// Antifrost — thirty-five hills for the cold season.
//
// Legend: `#` rock, `,` dirt, `.` tunnel, `@` player start,
// uppercase `W R Y G B` beds (W is the player's), lowercase `r y g b` wild ants.
//
// Par policy: twenty-eight hills carry their BFS optimum — found by
// exhaustive search, so no shorter win exists. That optimum is asserted
// by `solver::tests::solve_all_shipped`, which is `#[ignore]`d because
// a full re-solve outruns a test session's patience: it runs on demand
// (`cargo test --example antifrost --release -- --ignored`) and no
// shipped par moves without it. The replay that DOES run every test is
// the second proof of the same twenty-eight (a found win is replayed
// move by move against the real game). Seven hills — the five four-ant
// runs Deadfall, Winter Quarters, Deep Cellar, Ashfall, Ember Deep,
// plus Hearth Row and Ninefold — have spaces or lock-timings that
// outrun any cap we can carry, so their pars are routes that the
// `solver::tests::hand_route_wins_*` tests replay move by move in
// every run: beatable, not claimed unbeatable. `solver::hunt_route`
// (a layered beam search — sharp tunnel distances first, naive
// Manhattan as a second mind, `BEAM_WIDTH` 100k) re-hunts all seven
// at once under `solver::tests::hunt_the_unproven_seven`, now through
// the unified `solver::solve`: beam finds a route, bounded BFS tries
// to certify it — no cert has stuck yet, but two routes retimed:
// Ninefold's hand-worked 41 fell to a hunted 35 and Ashfall's 67 to
// 57 at the sharper score; Winter Quarters' 48 and Ember Deep's 71 —
// the last one unbeaten through three hunt generations — both fell
// when the beam widened to 100k (to 47 and 56).

